FDM patch 0001: integrator-aware ground friction (candidate 1 of session 03)

What it changes
  JSBSim sizes the ground friction forces so that each contact's slip velocity is zero after the
  next integration step (FGAccelerations::CalculateFrictionForces, Catto's method). v1.3.3 assumes
  that step is an Euler step: v + dt*vdot. But the rates are integrated by FGPropagate with a scheme
  chosen at run time, and the multistep schemes (trapezoidal, Adams-Bashforth 2 to 5) weight the new
  derivative by b0 (1.5 for AB2, 55/24 for AB4) and add past derivatives. The friction then
  overshoots, and the aircraft never settles at rest (REPORT-02, REVIEW-codex-02).

  JSBSim had integrator-aware friction in 2010 (commit 53709ba8, Bertrand Coconnier). It was moved
  and disabled in e67c5e42, friction came back in the Euler-only form in 85b3a574, and the disabled
  code was deleted in 40279c20 (2011). This patch restores the idea, adapted to v1.3.3.

How it is switched
  New property simulation/integrator/friction-aware (integer, default 0):
    0  JSBSim v1.3.3, unchanged. Every statement computes exactly what the unpatched code computes
       (checked: bit-identical logs, tests/native/verify_key.sh patched unpatched).
    1  Friction consistent with the schemes in use (candidate 1).
    2  The formula of the 2010 code, for comparison only.
  With Euler for both rates (and with dt = 0, at initialization) all three are the same.

Mode 1, the adaptation to v1.3.3
  Let d(n) be the derivative FGPropagate will integrate (vUVWidot in the ECI frame for translation,
  vPQRidot in the body frame for rotation) and b0, b1, ... the weights of its scheme. The step is
  rate + dt*(b0*d(n) + b1*d(n-1) + ...).
  - Right hand side: JSBSim's (slip + dt*vdot)/dt is the slip after an Euler step without friction.
    The multistep step differs from it by dt*((b0-1)*d(n) + b1*d(n-1) + ...), so that is added,
    for translation (rotated into the body frame) and for rotation, each with its own scheme.
  - Matrix: a friction force F changes the next translational rate by dt*b0t*F/m and the next
    rotational rate by dt*b0r*Jinv*(r x F). The translational and rotational parts of the system
    matrix are weighted by b0t and b0r. This keeps the solution exact when translation and rotation
    use different schemes (JSBSim's defaults are AB2 and Euler).
  Differences from the 2010 code: (a) 2010 divided the right hand side by b0 for translation and
  rotation separately and left the matrix alone, which is exact only when both use the same scheme;
  (b) 2010 used the body-frame derivative vUVWdot where the integrated quantity is the inertial one,
  which leaves a small bias on a rotating Earth; (c) the rotational history is now kept in the body
  frame, so it needs no rotation.

What it does not change
  FGPropagate (only four read-only accessors are added), the contact forces, the friction limits,
  the Gauss-Seidel solver, and everything else. Applied identically to the WebAssembly build and to
  the native answer key by scripts/lib-jsbsim-src.sh.

